The overlay's calls that take a work item are available below iOS 8, and hand its flags to the release: charon@apple-compat
carries dispatch_block_create and the rest of dispatch_block_* (packages/a/apple-compat/src/), so what Queue.swift,
Dispatch.swift and Source.swift marked iOS 8, and asked #available(iOS 8.0) for at run time, is the same code on every
release. Before this a barrier, a class of service or any other flag given to async(group:qos:flags:), sync(flags:),
asyncAfter or DispatchGroup.notify was dropped below 8, and the closure was dispatched plainly. The two marks of iOS 8 over
Queue.swift's class of service stay, and the paragraph after this one says why.

The release's libdispatch reads no block's flags, so the shims' calls to it go through apple-compat's charon_dispatch_async,
_sync, _group_async, _group_notify, _after and _source_set_event_handler, which submit a block made with
DISPATCH_BLOCK_BARRIER, and run a source's event handler made so, as a barrier, as libdispatch-703 (iOS 10) and later do,
and are the system's calls where the release has dispatch_block_create - which is what iOS 8 and 9 answer by, their
libdispatch having the block calls and no barrier bit for a source (libdispatch-442 and -500). What a class of service or a
voucher asks has no counterpart before iOS 8, as packages/a/apple-compat/src/dispatch_block.h says. A source's cancellation
and registration handlers are left as they are: on iOS 10 and later they run alone as well, the calls that reach them are
the release's own, and that is what coordination/crutches.md's entry for the barrier source handler records.

Two of Queue.swift's marks of iOS 8 stay: the #available over __dispatch_queue_attr_make_with_qos_class in
DispatchQueue.init(label:qos:attributes:autoreleaseFrequency:target:), and the @available on DispatchQueue.qos, whose body
calls __dispatch_queue_get_qos_class. The overlay reaches the release's libdispatch in both places and this header has no
shim's route beside them, and <dispatch/queue.h> marks both calls iOS 8.
Taking the marks down is what the compiler refuses, at the deployment target this runtime is built for:

  Queue.swift:162:11: error: '__dispatch_queue_attr_make_with_qos_class' is only available in iOS 8.0 or newer
  Queue.swift:475:44: error: '__dispatch_queue_get_qos_class' is only available in iOS 8.0 or newer

charon@apple-compat does answer both calls below 8 - dispatch_queue_attr_make_with_qos_class hands the attribute back as it
was given, so the queue is the serial or concurrent one it would have been, and dispatch_queue_get_qos_class says it has no
class (packages/a/apple-compat/src/dispatch_queue_attr_make_with_qos_class.c) - and a program that reaches either call
through C gets the release's answer. What stays marked is the API the release itself marks: below 8 a queue asked for a
class of service is given the attribute it was given, which is what init(label:qos:) does without the branch, and
DispatchQueue.qos is a property of iOS 8 and no earlier.

